# fdisk — real-world examples

## List partition tables on all disks

```sh
sudo fdisk -l
```

## Open a disk for partitioning

```sh
sudo fdisk /dev/sdd
```

## Interactive commands inside fdisk

| Key | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| p | print the partition table |
| d | delete a partition |
| l | list known partition types |
| n | create a new partition |
| t | change a partition's type |
| w | write changes and exit |
| q | quit without saving |

## Add a new physical disk end-to-end (new drive for /opt)

```sh
lsblk                      # identify the new disk
sudo fdisk /dev/sdd        # n (new partition, accept defaults), then w (write)
sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/sdd1    # create the filesystem
sudo blkid /dev/sdd1       # grab the UUID for fstab
```

Then add to `/etc/fstab` (0 0 disables fsck ordering and dump):

```text
UUID=<uuid-from-blkid>  /opt  xfs  defaults  0 0
```

```sh
sudo mount -a
df -h /opt
```

## Extend a physical disk (VM)

```sh
# 1. Unmount the drive and comment out its /etc/fstab entry
# 2. Grow the virtual disk in the hypervisor
sudo parted /dev/sdc print    # confirm current layout
sudo fdisk /dev/sdc           # p, d, n (accept defaults), w
sudo reboot
sudo e2fsck -f /dev/sdc1
sudo resize2fs /dev/sdc1
sudo mount /dev/sdc1 /data    # then uncomment the fstab entry
```

## Show a partition's size in blocks

```sh
sudo fdisk -s /dev/sdd1
```
